INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - MORNING

A cluttered, sunlit room. Pancakes on a griddle. Crayons and bills on the table. A dog's water bowl by the door.

GRANDMA PEARL HOLLOWAY, 70s, in a floral apron, flips pancakes with the economy of long practice. Her husband WALT, 70s, in suspenders, reads the paper at the head of the table and does not look up.

Their daughter LORNA, 40s, in scrubs, pours coffee into a travel mug while she packs a lunch. Her son DANNY, 12, shovels cereal. His younger sister ROSIE, 7, draws a horse on the back of a utility bill, tongue out.

PEARL
Danny, chew.

DANNY
I am chewing.

LORNA ^
Danny, chew with your mouth closed.

DANNY
I am!

Rosie holds up her drawing. Danny snatches it. Lorna lunges across the table to catch the cereal bowl before it tips, and Walt, without lowering the paper, hooks the sugar bowl out of range with one finger. Pearl slides a pancake onto Rosie's plate, flips another onto Danny's, and kisses Lorna's cheek on the way past. The dog barks once at the window. Nobody looks.

ROSIE
It's a horse.

DANNY
It's a potato with legs.

WALT
(from behind the paper)
It's a very good potato.

ROSIE
Grandpa!

PEARL
Walter, put the paper down.

WALT
In a minute.

PEARL
Walter.

He folds the paper. Looks at them over his glasses. They all stare.

WALT
What?

LORNA
(laughing)
Nothing, Dad.
